WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.570
An old leak, a new fault, and a changed appointment can arrive in one tenant email.

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:13.004
HausPost turns that thread into a repair brief your property coordinator can review, using Apertus running on your own infrastructure.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:21.572
Here, the quantized eight-billion-parameter model runs inside Docker on four CPU threads.

00:00:21.592 --> 00:00:25.671
We submit a synthetic German message through the live application.

00:00:25.691 --> 00:00:30.788
The waiting period is shortened; the result and timing come from that actual request.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.193
The tap is already repaired.

00:00:35.213 --> 00:00:37.557
The doorbell still needs attention.

00:00:37.577 --> 00:00:42.971
Wednesday’s appointment is cancelled, and Friday is a tenant request awaiting confirmation.

00:00:42.991 --> 00:00:47.748
Click a field to locate its original words, then check the meaning before approving it.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:52.599
The tenant left out the apartment number.

00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:55.708
HausPost prepares a German clarification.

00:00:55.728 --> 00:01:03.316
Once the coordinator supplies three B, the new detail is labelled as a coordinator entry, and the handoff can be reviewed again.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:10.975
Our six-case development set spans German, French, Italian, and English.

00:01:10.995 --> 00:01:16.653
It includes archived repairs, contradictory times, and an instruction- like footer.

00:01:16.673 --> 00:01:23.220
Raw answers, failed cases, and a repeatable evaluation are public, alongside the corrected workflow.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:30.052
Run make run from a clean checkout to start the full self-managed stack.

00:01:30.072 --> 00:01:33.846
The office keeps its own model and reviews every handoff.

00:01:33.866 --> 00:01:40.476
Open the example desk, explore the recorded results, and try HausPost with your own Apertus endpoint.
